<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José Matías Delgado</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1767–1832</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José Matías Delgado (1767–1832) was a Salvadoran priest and politician honoured as the father of the nation of El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a>. He was a leader of the independence movement from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> rising of 1811, opposed annexation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a>'s Mexican Empire after 1821, and presided over the constituent assembly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a> in 1823. He was named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of San Salvador, a disputed appointment.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-matias-delgado.jpg" alt="José Matías Delgado" width="320" height="370" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rafael Beltranena · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Painting_of_Jose_Matias_Delgado.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
